WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.501
This ancient creature, Vilevolodon diplomylus

00:00:08.501 --> 00:00:12.525
may have just solved a debate that 
puts the origin of mammals

00:00:12.525 --> 00:00:15.955
millions of years earlier than previously thought,

00:00:15.955 --> 00:00:19.157
and it did it with its ears.

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:38.214
So Vilevolodon actually means glider's&nbsp;
tooth, and this animal was a glider

00:00:38.214 --> 00:00:43.389
somewhat like a flying squirrel in terms of its size and&nbsp;lifestyle

00:00:43.389 --> 00:00:48.075
and the specimen that we are reporting preserves some features

00:00:48.075 --> 00:00:53.520
that the original specimen&nbsp;of Vilevolodon does not preserve very well.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:01:01.271
Vilevolodon is a haramiyid, it lived during the 
mesozoic era, the age of the dinosaurs.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:08.480
Until recently, haramiyids were only known from 
a few fossilised teeth and they remain enigmatic.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:13.438
Where do the haramiyids fall on the family tree of life of mammals?

00:01:13.438 --> 00:01:15.904
Or are they even mammals?

00:01:15.904 --> 00:01:22.137
So some scientists believe that the haramiyids actually&nbsp;
are inside of the group of mammals,

00:01:22.137 --> 00:01:26.836
including us and living monotremes and marsupials.

00:01:26.836 --> 00:01:30.971
But other&nbsp;scientists believe that they fall outside of that group,

00:01:30.971 --> 00:01:34.850
that they would not be technically&nbsp;called mammals,

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:37.058
they would be near mammals,

00:01:37.058 --> 00:01:44.540
and the controversy has really centered around how their&nbsp;ear is organized.

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:50.121
Ear bones are one of the defining traits used to identify mammals,

00:01:50.121 --> 00:01:53.840
and they're one of the keys to understand mammalian evolution.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:57.843
Modern mammals have a distinctive middle ear,

00:01:57.843 --> 00:02:01.192
made&nbsp;up of three small bones or ossicles.

00:02:01.192 --> 00:02:04.641
The malleus, incus, and stapes.

00:02:04.641 --> 00:02:08.800
These allow us to hear a broad&nbsp;
range of sound frequencies in great detail.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:13.717
But this wasn't the case for our evolutionary precursors.

00:02:13.717 --> 00:02:17.225
Ear bones originally formed part of the jaw.

00:02:17.225 --> 00:02:20.697
Here you can see two of the middle ear&nbsp;
bones; the malleus and incus,

00:02:20.697 --> 00:02:24.309
plus another part of the ear: the tympanic annulus.

00:02:24.309 --> 00:02:28.640
And as you go back&nbsp;in the fossil record you can see them changing.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:34.162
Some early animals had bones that were used for&nbsp;
both hearing and chewing

00:02:34.162 --> 00:02:40.321
and before that there were creatures that heard
the world entirely&nbsp;through their jaws.

00:02:40.321 --> 00:02:44.535
Paleontologists can therefore use the structure of the middle ear

00:02:44.535 --> 00:02:48.281
to tell them&nbsp;where along the evolutionary tree an animal sits.

00:02:48.281 --> 00:02:51.995
The group of scientists that have them falling&nbsp;outside of mammal,

00:02:51.995 --> 00:02:57.136
give them an ear that is a much more primitive type of ear,

00:02:57.136 --> 00:03:01.520
and the scientists that have the haramiyids falling inside of mammals

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:05.881
has the ear as being more advanced.

00:03:05.881 --> 00:03:09.032
When you&nbsp;look at our fossil under a microscope

00:03:09.032 --> 00:03:14.883
the lower jaw jumps out at you 
as a very identifiable&nbsp;structure,

00:03:14.883 --> 00:03:17.520
and situated behind the lower jaw

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:23.961
were some funny shaped little elements that we&nbsp;
were not a hundred percent sure what they were.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:30.211
So when we first saw them you know they were of&nbsp;
the size and general shape

00:03:30.211 --> 00:03:35.852
that we thought they might be two of the three middle ear ossicles

00:03:35.852 --> 00:03:41.918
but&nbsp;it wasn't until we actually looked at the CT scans of the specimen 

00:03:41.918 --> 00:03:48.480
that we were really able to say&nbsp;
"yes those are two of the three middle ear ossicles".

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:54.342
And on looking at them it was clear that they were&nbsp;very similar

00:03:54.342 --> 00:03:59.490
to the malleus and incus that occur in living monotremes,

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:05.120
the egg laying mammals which&nbsp;
today are represented by the duck-billed platypus

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:09.055
and the spiny anteater or echidna.

00:04:09.055 --> 00:04:15.418
Because haramiyids are so ancient placing them within mammals pushes the origin of mammals as a group

00:04:15.418 --> 00:04:20.781
back to the late Triassic, about 200 million years ago.

00:04:20.781 --> 00:04:23.894
But this might not be the end of the story.

00:04:23.894 --> 00:04:28.156
You know I think the debate has an answer today,

00:04:28.156 --> 00:04:32.393
but tomorrow when somebody finds another&nbsp;haramiyid,

00:04:32.393 --> 00:04:32.893
or another fossil that has impact on the position of haramiyids,

00:04:32.893 --> 00:04:44.574
that debate will start&nbsp;up again and we'll cycle through this again.

